ACL:
            Vault entities: Using Identity secrets engine, An entity is a representation of a single person or system used to log into Vault. 
            Each has a unique value. Each entity is made up of zero or more aliases
            Alias is a combination of the auth method plus some identification. It is a mapping between an entity and auth method(s)
            can be automatically or manually created
            An entity can be manually created to map multiple entities for a single user to provide more efficient authorization management. By doing this teh Token inherits capabilities granted by both policies
            Create and entity:
                        vault write identity/entity name="Julie Smith" \
                        policies="it-management" \
                        metadata="organization"="HCVOP, Inc" \
                        metadata="team"="management"
            Add an Alias to an Entity:
                        Add GitHub auth as an alias
                        $ vault write identity/entity-alias name="jsmith22" \
                        canonical_id=<entity_id> \
                        mount_accessor=<github_auth_accessor>
                        Note: canonical_id is the entity id that we get from step 1 when we had created entity and mount_accessor we cxan get by running "vault auth list"
            Vault Groups:
                        A group can contain multiple entities as its members.
                        A group can also have subgroups.
            Vault Policies:
                        In JSON or HCL. Policies are attached to a token. A token can have multiple policies.
                        Policies are cumulative and capabilities are additive.
                        root policy is created by default – superuser with all permissions. You cannot change nor delete this policy. Attached to all root tokens.
                        default policy is created by default – provides common permissions. You can change this policy, but it cannot be deleted. Attached to all non-root tokens by default (can be removed if needed)
                        Many paths in Vault require a root token or sudo capability to use
                        Capabilities:-
                                    sudo: Allows access to paths that are root-protected
                        The plus (+) supports wildcard matching for a single directory in the path 
                        ACL Templating: Use variable replacement in some policy strings with values available to the token
                                    Eg: path "secret/data/{{identity.entity.id}}/*" {
                                    capabilities = ["create", "update", "read", "delete"]}
            Sentinel Policies:
                        Sentinel is an embeddable policy as code framework
                        Eg time based access to certain paths for eg 9 to 5.
                        Types of Sentinel Policies :
                                    Role Governing Policies (RGPs): Sentinel policies that are tied to tokens, identity entities, or identity groups
                                    Endpoint Governing Policies (EGPs): Sentinel policies that are tied to paths instead of tokens
                        Enforcement Levels:-
                                    Enforcement Levels: Advisory (as a warning only), Soft Mandatory (use the –policy-override flag to overwride) , Hard Mandatory
                                    Understand Policy evaluation flow: Is request authenticated -> yes -> evaluate acl ploiciies, rgp, egp
                                                                         Is request authenticated -> no -> evaluate egp
            Control groups: Control groups add an additional authorization requirement on configured paths
                        Control Group requirements can be specified in either ACL policies or within a Sentinel policy
                        Currently, the only supported Control Group factor is an Identity Group
                        Vault returns a wrapping token – rather than the requested secrets
                        The authorizers defined in the control group policy must approve the request


