Namespaces:
            Multi-tenant but centralized management
            The default namespace is ‘root’
            Tokens are only valid in a single namespace, but you can create an entity who has access to other namespaces
            Using Namespaces on the CLI:
                        export VAULT_NAMESPACE=<namespace>
                        vault kv get –namespace=<namespace> kv/data/sql/prod
            Referencing Namespaces in the API: 
                        X-Vault-Namespace
                        Add the Namespace to the API Endpoint after ...v1/<ns>/...
            We can Authenticating to a given Namespace via UI
            Authenticating to a Namespace via CLI: vault login -namespace=cloud-team -method=userpass username=xyz
            Enabling an Auth Method In a Namespace: vault auth enable -namespace=cloud-team userpass

Rekey Vault and Rotate Encryption Keys:
            Rekey: Creates a new set of Vault recovery/unseal keys
            Provides a nonce value to be given to key holders
            The Rekey operation is an online operation and can be done anytime

            Key Rotation: Rotates the encryption key that is used to protect data on the storage backend
                          New keys are added to the keyring - old values can still be decrypted with the old key
                          the encryption key is never available to users or operators
                          Permissions Needed for Key Rotation: The sys/rotate endpoint is a root protected API endpoint. This means that you either need a root token or sudo privileges on the sys/rotate path
                                                                path "sys/rotate" {
                                                                        capabilities = ["update","sudo"]
                                                                        }
                                                            path "sys/key-status" {
                                                            capabilities = ["read"]
                                                                        } # Needed to read the status of the key after rotating it, not to rotate it

Cubbyhole secret engine:
            Cubbyhole Secrets Engine is used to store arbitrary secrets. Enabled by default at the cubbyhole/ path
            Its lifetime is linked to the token used to write the data. No concept of a time-to-live (TTL) or refresh interval for values in cubbyhole
            Even the root token cannot read the data if it wasn't written by the root
            Cubbyhole Secrets Engine cannot be disabled, moved, or enabled multiple times
            Each service token gets its own cubbyhole. Cubbyhole expires when the token does
            Writing and Read Data to Cubbyhole via CLI: vault write cubbyhole/training certification=hcvop
                                                         vault read cubbyhole/training
            Instead of sending secrets over the network, you can use cubbyhole's response wrapping feature
            

            

